    "consulting_editor": "Lynn R. Hovda •Control of cerebral edema with mannitol 500 mg/kg q6h slow IV bolus, dexamethasone 1–2 mg/kg q6h IV, and furosemide 1–2 mg/kg q6h IV may have limited efficacy. •Diazepam 0.5–1 mg/kg, IV as needed, or other anticonvulsants may be given to abolish severe muscle tremors and seizures. •20% IV lipid emulsions (ILE)—bolus dose 1.5 mL/kg over 2–3 minutes; CRI of 0.25 mL/kg/min for 30–60 minutes. Check serum q2h until it becomes clear; repeat as needed; if no clinical improvement after 3 doses, discontinue. Other ILE dosing regimens can be used. CONTRAINDICATIONS/POSSIBLE INTERACTIONS •Contraindications for the use of mannitol include renal disease, pulmonary edema, dehydration, and intracranial hemorrhage. •Animals receiving mannitol therapy may become dehydrated. •Rehydration may be associated with a worsening of clinical signs, possibly due to FOLLOW-UP MISCELLANEOUS rodenticide toxicoses in small animals. Vet Clin North Am Small Anim Pract 2018, 48:1027–1038.",
